var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"location":"trees/#Species-trees","page":"Species trees","title":"Species trees","text":"A SpeciesTree holds the parent/child hierarchy of species: each child is a more resolved state of its parent, obtained by filling in one more field. Bulk N2 has the child N2[X], which has the children N2[X,vib=0], N2[X,vib=1], and so on.\n\nThe tree exists so that a chemistry can be written at one level of detail and solved at another. Reactions are written over whatever species the data source names — often lumped, e + N2[X] --> ... — while the model integrates the leaves. apply_tree bridges the two.","category":"section"},{"location":"trees/#Building","page":"Species trees","title":"Building","text":"Constructing from a species inserts its whole ancestor chain, found by repeated get_parent_species. Constructing from several merges those chains, sharing every common ancestor. The root is a node holding nothing.\n\nusing PlasmaSpecies\nt = SpeciesTree([\"e\", \"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\", \"N2[X,vib=2]\", \"N2[+]\"])\n\nAccepted arguments: a Species or String, a vector of either, or varargs. Add to an existing tree with push!, or combine two with merge!:\n\npush!(t, \"N2[X,vib=3]\")\n\nt[sp] = subtree grafts a separately built branch onto an existing tree, replacing the node for sp value and children alike.","category":"section"},{"location":"trees/#Querying","page":"Species trees","title":"Querying","text":"leaves(t)\nt[\"N2[X]\"]\nmatching_leaves(t, Species(\"N2[X,vib=0-1]\"))\nmatching_leaves(t, Species(\"O2\"))\n\nleaves gives the most resolved states — the model's unknowns. Indexing finds one node and returns its subtree, stopping at the first match. matching_leaves is the pattern query: it descends every branch and collects the leaves under each node that the given species matches, so a wildcard field or a vib=0-4 range expands to every level actually present.\n\nThe tree implements AbstractTrees, so print_tree, Leaves and the rest of that interface work directly on it.","category":"section"},{"location":"trees/#Energies","page":"Species trees","title":"Energies","text":"apply_energy! fills the energy field of every leaf from a function of its state labels:\n\ntv = SpeciesTree([\"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\", \"N2[X,vib=2]\"])\napply_energy!(tv, q -> 0.29 * q.vib)   # eV, harmonic\nenergy.(leaves(tv))\n\nThe callback receives a named tuple (elec, vib, rot); the unit is the caller's choice, and only reaction_energy and thermal_source_term consume it. Leaves are rebuilt with with_fields, so every other field survives.\n\npush!, merge!, getindex and setindex! are extended for SpeciesTree as described above; their docstrings are available at the REPL.","category":"section"},{"location":"trees/#PlasmaSpecies.SpeciesTree-trees","page":"Species trees","title":"PlasmaSpecies.SpeciesTree","text":"SpeciesTree(sp::Species)\nSpeciesTree(str::String)\nSpeciesTree(species)          # Vector or varargs of Species / String\n\nThe species hierarchy: a node holds one Species (or nothing for the root) and its children, each a more resolved state of its parent.\n\nBuilding from a species inserts its whole ancestor chain, obtained by repeated get_parent_species, so \"N2[X,vib=1]\" alone yields nothing → N2 → N2[X] → N2[X,vib=1]. Building from several species merges those chains, sharing every common ancestor.\n\njulia> SpeciesTree([\"e\", \"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\", \"N2[+]\"])\nnothing\n├─ e\n├─ N2\n│  └─ N2[X]\n│     ├─ N2[X,vib=0]\n│     └─ N2[X,vib=1]\n└─ N2[+]\n\nThe tree implements AbstractTrees, so print_tree, Leaves and friends work on it. Supported operations: push! and merge! to add species, t[sp] / t[\"N2[X]\"] to fetch a subtree (nothing if absent), t[sp] = subtree to replace one, leaves and matching_leaves to collect states, and apply_energy! to fill in level energies.\n\nThe leaves are the states a model actually integrates; interior nodes are the lumped species reactions may be written over. apply_tree uses that to expand a reaction written for N2[X] into one per vibrational level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"trees/#PlasmaSpecies.leaves-trees","page":"Species trees","title":"PlasmaSpecies.leaves","text":"leaves(t::SpeciesTree) -> Vector{Species}\n\nEvery leaf species of t, in tree order. These are the most resolved states in the hierarchy — the ones a model carries as unknowns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"trees/#PlasmaSpecies.matching_leaves-trees","page":"Species trees","title":"PlasmaSpecies.matching_leaves","text":"matching_leaves(t::SpeciesTree, formula::Species) -> Vector{Species}\n\nCollect every leaf of t matched by formula (via nodevalue(c) in formula, see Base.in/species_matches), descending into every branch rather than stopping at the first match like t[formula]/Base.getindex does. Whenever formula matches a node — exactly, or via a UnitRange vibrational/rotational field containing that node's level — the whole subtree's leaves are taken and that branch is not searched further; otherwise the search continues into the node's children. This is what lets a reaction formula species written with a vibrational range (e.g. vib=0-4) expand, in apply_tree, to every matching level actually present in the tree, even when those levels are separate sibling nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"trees/#PlasmaSpecies.apply_energy!-trees","page":"Species trees","title":"PlasmaSpecies.apply_energy!","text":"apply_energy!(t::SpeciesTree, energy) -> SpeciesTree\n\nSet the energy field of every leaf of t in place. energy is called with a named tuple (elec, vib, rot) of that leaf's state labels and returns its level energy; the unit is the caller's choice.\n\napply_energy!(t, q -> ωe * (q.vib + 0.5) - ωeχe * (q.vib + 0.5)^2)\n\nLeaves are replaced via with_fields, so every other field survives. Interior nodes are left alone. reaction_energy consumes what this sets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#API","page":"API","title":"API","text":"Every documented name in PlasmaSpecies, exported or not.","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Index","page":"API","title":"Index","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Species-and-quantum-labels","page":"API","title":"Species and quantum labels","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Charge-states","page":"API","title":"Charge states","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Electronic-state-types","page":"API","title":"Electronic-state types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Gases-and-formulas","page":"API","title":"Gases and formulas","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Nuclide-reference-data","page":"API","title":"Nuclide reference data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Tree-types","page":"API","title":"Tree types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Reaction-types","page":"API","title":"Reaction types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Catalyst-export","page":"API","title":"Catalyst export","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.QuantumLabel","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.QuantumLabel","text":"QuantumLabel\n\nThe closed set of forms vibrational_state/rotational_state can take: a single quantum number (Int), several quantum numbers (Tuple/NamedTuple, e.g. normal modes of a polyatomic), a contiguous range of levels lumped together for reaction purposes (UnitRange{Int}, see level_matches/matching_leaves), or an opaque hand-picked label (Symbol) with no containment semantics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Species","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Species","text":"Species(; gas, charge=Neutral(), electronic_state=nothing,\n          vibrational_state=nothing, rotational_state=nothing,\n          energy=nothing, degeneracy=nothing, metadata=nothing)\n\nOne state of one gas. All fields are type parameters, so a Species is concretely typed and cheap to store in arrays and Dict keys.\n\nUsually built from a LoKI-B string instead — see Species(::String).\n\nIdentity fields\n\nCompared by == and hashed. Together they name the state:\n\ngas: the parent gas. Normally a Molecule resolved from a chemical formula (see parse_formula), otherwise Electron or the StringGas fallback.\ncharge: Neutral (default), Positive(n) or Negative(n).\nelectronic_state: an ElectronicState, or nothing for unresolved.\nvibrational_state, rotational_state: a QuantumLabel, or nothing. By convention a resolved level implies its parent is resolved too (rot without vib is meaningless), but this is not enforced.\n\nnothing means unresolved, not zero: N2 is bulk N₂, N2[X,vib=0] is its ground vibrational level, and the two are different species that a SpeciesTree relates as parent and child.\n\nPayload fields\n\nIgnored by == and hash, carried through with_fields:\n\nenergy: level energy, used by reaction_energy. Set in bulk with apply_energy!.\ndegeneracy: statistical weight of the level.\nmetadata: opaque payload for downstream packages.\n\nThe split is deliberate: energy/degeneracy/metadata are annotations on a state, not part of its identity, so attaching them never breaks a Dict{Species,V} lookup.\n\nAn Electron gas always carries Negative(1); any charge passed for one is normalised to that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Species-Tuple{String}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Species","text":"Species(str::String; metadata=nothing) -> Species\n\nParse a species string in the notation of the LoKI-B Boltzmann solver:\n\nGAS[CHARGE,ELECTRONIC,vib=VIB,rot=ROT]\n\nEvery bracketed field is optional, but the order is fixed. GAS is resolved by Gas — a chemical formula, \"e\", or an unresolvable label kept as a StringGas. CHARGE is a run of +/- (see Charge), the electronic state is parsed by ElectronicState, and the vib=/rot= levels by parse_quantum_label. Whitespace is stripped first.\n\nSpecies(\"e\")\nSpecies(\"N2\")                 # bulk N₂, no state resolved\nSpecies(\"N2[+]\")\nSpecies(\"N2[+,B]\")\nSpecies(\"N2[X,vib=0,rot=10]\")\nSpecies(\"N2[X,vib=0-4]\")      # a lumped range of levels\nSpecies(\"CO2[X,vib=(0,1,0)]\") # normal modes of a polyatomic\nSpecies(\"16O-1H\")             # a specific isotopologue\n\nThe gas token accepts letters, digits and -, so ExoMol-style isotope slugs stay one token. Anything else — an underscore, a stray bracket — throws: \"N2[+\" is a typo, not a neutral N₂.\n\nmetadata is stored as-is and never inspected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#Base.in-Tuple{Species, Species}","page":"API","title":"Base.in","text":"candidate::Species in formula::Species -> Bool\n\nWhether candidate is matched by formula — an alias for species_matches(formula, candidate), exposed via ∈/in to mirror SpectraUtils's Base.in(child::State, parent::State) (e.g. s in boltzmann.parent). nothing/range fields on formula act as wildcards/ containment checks, same as species_matches; reflexive (sp in sp is true), unlike is_parent_species which is a strict-ancestor check and doesn't compare rotational_state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies._quantum_label_isless-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies._quantum_label_isless","text":"_quantum_label_isless(a, b)\n\nOrder two QuantumLabel values. Uses isless directly where Base defines it (Int, Tuple, Symbol); falls back to comparing string(a)/string(b) for pairs without a natural order (mixed types, NamedTuple) so Species sorting stays total and never throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.charge-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.charge","text":"charge(sp::Species) -> Charge\n\nThe charge state. See Charge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.degeneracy-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.degeneracy","text":"degeneracy(sp::Species)\n\nStatistical weight of the level, or nothing if unset. Not part of species identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.electronic_state-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.electronic_state","text":"electronic_state(sp::Species) -> Union{ElectronicState,Nothing}\n\nThe electronic state, or nothing if unresolved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.energy-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.energy","text":"energy(sp::Species)\n\nLevel energy, or nothing if unset. Not part of species identity; set it with with_fields or, for a whole tree, apply_energy!. Units are whatever the caller used — reaction_energy only subtracts them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.format_quantum_label-Tuple{UnitRange{Int64}}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.format_quantum_label","text":"format_quantum_label(x) -> String\n\nInverse of parse_quantum_label, used by Base.show(::Species) so vib=/rot= round-trip through display back to the same parsed value. Only needed for UnitRange, whose default string uses \"a:b\" (0:4) rather than the \"a-b\" (0-4) syntax parse_quantum_label accepts; every other QuantumLabel variant's default string is already what the parser expects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.gas-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.gas","text":"gas(sp::Species) -> Gas\n\nThe parent gas. See Gas.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.get_parent_species-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.get_parent_species","text":"get_parent_species(sp::Species) -> Union{Species,Nothing}\n\nStrip the most specific resolved state field and return the resulting species: rot first, then vib, then the electronic state. Returns nothing for a species that has none of them, i.e. the root of its branch.\n\nApplied repeatedly by SpeciesTree to build the chain of ancestors above a species. Payload fields (energy, degeneracy, metadata) are not carried over — a parent is a different state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.is_parent_species-Tuple{Species, Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.is_parent_species","text":"is_parent_species(sp::Species, parent::Species) -> Bool\n\nWhether parent is a strict ancestor of sp in the species hierarchy: same gas and charge, electronic and vibrational state matched via level_matches (so nothing on parent acts as a wildcard), and parent != sp. rotational_state is deliberately not compared. Unlike species_matches, this is irreflexive — a species is not its own parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.isnegative-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.isnegative","text":"isnegative(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x is a negatively charged Species. Dispatches on the charge type parameter, so it resolves at compile time. false for a non-Species.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.isneutral-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.isneutral","text":"isneutral(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x is an uncharged Species. See isnegative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.ispositive-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ispositive","text":"ispositive(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x is a positively charged Species. See isnegative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.level_matches-Tuple{Nothing, Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.level_matches","text":"level_matches(formula_value, candidate_value) -> Bool\n\nWhether a single field (electronic/vibrational/rotational state) of a formula Species matches a concrete candidate value. nothing on the formula side is a wildcard (matches anything, as used by is_parent_species for hierarchy matching); a UnitRange{Int} on the formula side matches any Int it contains (used by matching_leaves to expand reactions written over a range of vibrational levels); everything else falls back to exact equality — including Symbol labels, which are deliberately opaque (no containment).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.mass-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.mass","text":"mass(sp::Species) -> Float64\n\nMass in kg, derived from the gas composition with the charge accounted for: n missing electrons for Positive(n), n extra for Negative(n). An electron species returns the electron rest mass directly.\n\nErrors if the gas is a StringGas, i.e. if the label never resolved to a chemical formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.metadata-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.metadata","text":"metadata(sp::Species)\n\nOpaque payload attached by a downstream package, or nothing. Not part of species identity, and never inspected by this package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.parse_quantum_label-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.parse_quantum_label","text":"parse_quantum_label(s) -> QuantumLabel\n\nParse the string form of a vibrational_state/rotational_state field into a QuantumLabel. \"3\" becomes an Int, \"0-4\" a UnitRange{Int}, \"(1,0,2)\" a Tuple, \"(v1=1,v2=0)\" a NamedTuple, and anything else an opaque Symbol. nothing passes through unchanged. Inverse of format_quantum_label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.rotational_state-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.rotational_state","text":"rotational_state(sp::Species) -> Union{QuantumLabel,Nothing}\n\nThe rotational level, or nothing if unresolved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.species_matches-Tuple{Species, Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.species_matches","text":"species_matches(formula::Species, candidate::Species) -> Bool\n\nWhether candidate is matched by formula: gas/charge compared exactly, electronic/vibrational/rotational state compared via level_matches (so nothing/range fields on formula act as wildcards/containment checks). Used by matching_leaves to expand a reaction formula species — which may specify a range of vibrational levels — into every matching leaf of a SpeciesTree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.vibrational_state-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.vibrational_state","text":"vibrational_state(sp::Species) -> Union{QuantumLabel,Nothing}\n\nThe vibrational level, or nothing if unresolved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.with_fields-Tuple{Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.with_fields","text":"with_fields(sp::Species; kwargs...) -> Species\n\nCopy sp, replacing only the fields named in kwargs. Everything else — energy, degeneracy and the opaque metadata payload downstream packages attach — is carried over.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Charge","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Charge","text":"Charge\n\nCharge state of a Species: Positive, Negative or Neutral. Separate types rather than a signed integer, so charge can be dispatched on.\n\nCharge(s::AbstractString) -> Charge\nCharge(::Nothing) -> Neutral()\n\nParse the charge field of a LoKI-B species string: a run of + or - gives Positive(n)/Negative(n), an empty string or \"0\" gives Neutral(). Anything else throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Negative","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Negative","text":"Negative(n) <: Charge\n\nn-fold negatively charged, written as n - signs (\"O[-]\"). An Electron species is always Negative(1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Neutral","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Neutral","text":"Neutral() <: Charge\n\nUncharged. The default when a species string carries no charge field, and printed as the empty string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Positive","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Positive","text":"Positive(n) <: Charge\n\nn-fold positively charged, written as n + signs (\"N2[+]\", \"N2[++]\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.to_value-Tuple{Positive}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.to_value","text":"to_value(c::Charge) -> Int\n\nSigned charge number: +n, -n or 0. Used by ischargebalanced and by mass to count missing/extra electrons. Not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.ElectronicState","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ElectronicState","text":"ElectronicState\n\nElectronic state of a Species. Two concrete types: StringElectronicState (a bare letter label) and TermSymbol (label plus spin/Λ/parity). Equality and ordering use label only, so the two are interchangeable wherever a state is matched.\n\nElectronicState(x) dispatches on the argument: a string is parsed (see ElectronicState(::AbstractString)), nothing passes through, and an existing state is returned unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.ElectronicState-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ElectronicState","text":"ElectronicState(x::AbstractString) -> ElectronicState\n\nParse an electronic-state label. Plain LoKI-B labels (\"X\", \"A\", \"a''\", no digit suffix) become a StringElectronicState. Fused term-symbol strings in the format ExoMol's ElecState column uses — <label><multiplicity><Λ-name><parity>[<reflection>], e.g. \"B3Pig\", \"A3Sigmau+\" — decompose into a TermSymbol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.StringElectronicState","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.StringElectronicState","text":"StringElectronicState <: ElectronicState\n\nAn electronic state carrying only its conventional letter label (:X, :A, :a'', ...) and no term-symbol physics. This is what ElectronicState(::AbstractString) returns for plain LoKI-B labels; strings that decompose into spin/Λ/parity become a TermSymbol instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.TermSymbol","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.TermSymbol","text":"TermSymbol <: ElectronicState\n\nAn electronic state carrying real term-symbol data alongside its conventional label: spin multiplicity (2S+1), orbital angular momentum projection Λ (0=Σ, 1=Π, 2=Δ, 3=Φ, 4=Γ), parity (:g/:u), and reflection symmetry (:plus/:minus, meaningful for Σ states, and not always available even then). All but label are optional — real data doesn't always carry all of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.label","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.label","text":"label(s::ElectronicState) -> Symbol\n\nThe conventional letter label of an electronic state (:X, :A, :B, :a'', ...). This is what identifies/matches a state everywhere else in the package (LoKI-B parsing round-trip, ==, tree lookup, sorting) — richer physics carried by TermSymbol is additive annotation on top of it, the same way Species's energy/degeneracy don't participate in Species equality either.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.REGISTERED_GASES","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.REGISTERED_GASES","text":"REGISTERED_GASES\n\nLabels that are not chemical formulas and so cannot be resolved by parse_formula. Only the electron qualifies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"constant"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Gas","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Gas","text":"Gas\n\nThe chemical identity of a Species, independent of its charge and internal state. Three concrete types: Molecule (resolved from a chemical formula, knows its composition and mass), Electron, and StringGas (unresolvable label, no mass).\n\nConstruct with Gas(label); gases sort by their printed label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Gas-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Gas","text":"Gas(label) -> Gas\n\nResolve a gas label, in three steps:\n\na special, non-formula label from REGISTERED_GASES — currently just \"e\";\notherwise a chemical formula, via parse_formula, giving a Molecule that knows its own mass;\notherwise StringGas, so an unrecognised label still parses, prints and round-trips — it just cannot be weighed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.StringGas","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.StringGas","text":"StringGas(name) <: Gas\n\nFallback for a label that is not a resolvable chemical formula. Behaves like any other gas except that it has no composition, and therefore no mass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.composition-Tuple{Gas}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.composition","text":"composition(g::Gas)\n\nFallback: only a Molecule carries a composition. Errors for StringGas and Electron.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.mass-Tuple{Gas}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.mass","text":"mass(g::Gas) -> Float64\n\nMass of one particle in kg, summed over the composition. Errors for a StringGas, whose label never resolved to a formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Molecule","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Molecule","text":"Molecule(composition) <: Gas\n\nA gas defined by what it is made of: a tuple of nuclide => count pairs in the order they were written. Mass and both display forms derive from the composition, so any formula that resolves against the nuclide tables is a usable gas.\n\nprint preserves the written order (H2O, not OH2), while == and hash use the canonical composition, so OH and HO are one gas and one Dict key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.canonical_composition-Tuple{Molecule}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.canonical_composition","text":"canonical_composition(g) -> Tuple of `nuclide => count` pairs\n\nComposition with repeated nuclides merged and sorted by (atomic number, mass number). This is the identity used by == and hash.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.composition-Tuple{Molecule}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.composition","text":"composition(g) -> Tuple of `nuclide => count` pairs\n\nWritten-order composition of a gas. Errors for gases that have no resolved composition (StringGas).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.parse_formula-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.parse_formula","text":"parse_formula(s) -> Union{Molecule,Nothing}\n\nParse a chemical formula into a Molecule, or return nothing if it does not resolve — unknown element symbol, unknown isotope, or leftover text. Returning nothing rather than throwing lets Gas fall back to StringGas.\n\nThree spellings are accepted:\n\nNatural abundance: \"Ar\", \"N2\", \"CO2\", \"H2O\", \"CH4\". Every symbol resolves to an Element, i.e. the standard atomic weight.\nA single isotopic segment: \"14N2\", \"16O\".\nExoMol iso_slug: \"16O-1H\", \"12C-16O2\", \"28Si-16O\" — segments joined by -. Matches the spelling ExoMol.jl uses, so species strings line up with load_isotopologue slugs.\n\nTwo-letter symbols match greedily, per the usual chemical convention: \"CO\" is carbon monoxide while \"Co\" is cobalt, \"NO\" is N + O while \"No\" is nobelium. Case is significant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.M_E_KG","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.M_E_KG","text":"M_E_KG\n\nThe electron rest mass in kilograms, CODATA 2018 — the same source as U_KG and the nuclide tables, so an ion mass stays consistent with the neutral it is derived from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"constant"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Electron","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Electron","text":"Electron <: Gas\n\nThe electron. The only entry in REGISTERED_GASES, so the label \"e\" is resolved before any formula parsing.\n\nA Species built on it is forced to Negative(1), and mass returns the electron rest mass directly instead of subtracting it from a neutral.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.U_KG","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.U_KG","text":"U_KG\n\nThe unified atomic mass unit (dalton) in kilograms, CODATA 2018.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"constant"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Element","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Element","text":"Element(symbol, Z, weight) <: Nuclide\n\nA chemical element at natural isotopic abundance. weight is the standard atomic weight in daltons (IUPAC 2021 conventional values).\n\nA bare element symbol in a formula resolves to this, so \"N2\" is two natural-abundance nitrogens (2 × 14.007 u), which is what LoKI-B and BOLSIG+ assume for a bulk gas. Give a mass number (\"14N2\") for a single nuclide.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Isotope","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Isotope","text":"Isotope(symbol, Z, A, mass) <: Nuclide\n\nA single nuclide. mass is the exact atomic mass in daltons (AME2020).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.Nuclide","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Nuclide","text":"Nuclide\n\nA composition primitive: either an Element at natural isotopic abundance or a specific Isotope. Gases are compositions over nuclides — see Molecule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.atomic_number-Tuple{Nuclide}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.atomic_number","text":"atomic_number(n::Nuclide) -> Int\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.mass-Tuple{Element}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.mass","text":"mass(n::Nuclide) -> Float64\n\nMass of one atom in kilograms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.mass_number-Tuple{Element}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.mass_number","text":"mass_number(n::Nuclide) -> Union{Int,Nothing}\n\nNucleon count for an Isotope, nothing for an Element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.symbol-Tuple{Element}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.symbol","text":"symbol(n::Nuclide) -> Symbol\n\nElement symbol, with no mass number attached (:O for both O and ¹⁶O).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.ELEMENTS","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ELEMENTS","text":"ELEMENTS::Dict{Symbol,Element}\n\nEvery chemical element at natural isotopic abundance, keyed by symbol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"constant"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.ELEMENT_ALIASES","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ELEMENT_ALIASES","text":"ELEMENT_ALIASES\n\nSymbols that stand for a specific nuclide rather than an element: deuterium and tritium. \"D2\" and \"HT\" therefore resolve without a mass number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"constant"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.ISOTOPES","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ISOTOPES","text":"ISOTOPES::Dict{Tuple{Symbol,Int},Isotope}\n\nExact nuclide masses, keyed by (element symbol, mass number). Add a line here for anything missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"constant"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.element-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.element","text":"element(sym) -> Union{Element,Isotope,Nothing}\n\nLook up a bare symbol: an Element at natural abundance, an Isotope for an ELEMENT_ALIASES entry such as :D, or nothing if unknown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.isotope-Tuple{Symbol, Integer}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.isotope","text":"isotope(sym, A) -> Union{Isotope,Nothing}\n\nLook up a nuclide by element symbol and mass number, or nothing if it is not in ISOTOPES.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.SpeciesTree","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.SpeciesTree","text":"SpeciesTree(sp::Species)\nSpeciesTree(str::String)\nSpeciesTree(species)          # Vector or varargs of Species / String\n\nThe species hierarchy: a node holds one Species (or nothing for the root) and its children, each a more resolved state of its parent.\n\nBuilding from a species inserts its whole ancestor chain, obtained by repeated get_parent_species, so \"N2[X,vib=1]\" alone yields nothing → N2 → N2[X] → N2[X,vib=1]. Building from several species merges those chains, sharing every common ancestor.\n\njulia> SpeciesTree([\"e\", \"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\", \"N2[+]\"])\nnothing\n├─ e\n├─ N2\n│  └─ N2[X]\n│     ├─ N2[X,vib=0]\n│     └─ N2[X,vib=1]\n└─ N2[+]\n\nThe tree implements AbstractTrees, so print_tree, Leaves and friends work on it. Supported operations: push! and merge! to add species, t[sp] / t[\"N2[X]\"] to fetch a subtree (nothing if absent), t[sp] = subtree to replace one, leaves and matching_leaves to collect states, and apply_energy! to fill in level energies.\n\nThe leaves are the states a model actually integrates; interior nodes are the lumped species reactions may be written over. apply_tree uses that to expand a reaction written for N2[X] into one per vibrational level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#Base.getindex-Tuple{SpeciesTree, Species}","page":"API","title":"Base.getindex","text":"t[sp::Species] -> Union{SpeciesTree,Nothing}\nt[str::String] -> Union{SpeciesTree,Nothing}\n\nDepth-first search for the node holding sp, returned as a subtree, or nothing if it is not in t. Stops at the first match — use matching_leaves to collect every state a wildcard or range matches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#Base.merge!-Tuple{SpeciesTree, SpeciesTree}","page":"API","title":"Base.merge!","text":"merge!(left::SpeciesTree, right::SpeciesTree...) -> SpeciesTree\n\nMerge right into left in place, recursing wherever both hold the same species so shared ancestors are not duplicated. Returns left.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#Base.push!-Tuple{SpeciesTree, Any}","page":"API","title":"Base.push!","text":"push!(t::SpeciesTree, x) -> SpeciesTree\n\nAdd a species (or string) and its ancestor chain to t. Equivalent to merge!(t, SpeciesTree(x)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#Base.setindex!-Tuple{SpeciesTree, SpeciesTree, Species}","page":"API","title":"Base.setindex!","text":"t[sp] = subtree\n\nReplace the node holding sp in t with the node holding sp in subtree, value and children alike. Throws KeyError if sp is missing from either. Used to graft a separately built branch (e.g. a full vibrational ladder) onto an existing tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.apply_energy!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.apply_energy!","text":"apply_energy!(t::SpeciesTree, energy) -> SpeciesTree\n\nSet the energy field of every leaf of t in place. energy is called with a named tuple (elec, vib, rot) of that leaf's state labels and returns its level energy; the unit is the caller's choice.\n\napply_energy!(t, q -> ωe * (q.vib + 0.5) - ωeχe * (q.vib + 0.5)^2)\n\nLeaves are replaced via with_fields, so every other field survives. Interior nodes are left alone. reaction_energy consumes what this sets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.leaves-Tuple{SpeciesTree}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.leaves","text":"leaves(t::SpeciesTree) -> Vector{Species}\n\nEvery leaf species of t, in tree order. These are the most resolved states in the hierarchy — the ones a model carries as unknowns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.matching_leaves-Tuple{SpeciesTree, Species}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.matching_leaves","text":"matching_leaves(t::SpeciesTree, formula::Species) -> Vector{Species}\n\nCollect every leaf of t matched by formula (via nodevalue(c) in formula, see Base.in/species_matches), descending into every branch rather than stopping at the first match like t[formula]/Base.getindex does. Whenever formula matches a node — exactly, or via a UnitRange vibrational/rotational field containing that node's level — the whole subtree's leaves are taken and that branch is not searched further; otherwise the search continues into the node's children. This is what lets a reaction formula species written with a vibrational range (e.g. vib=0-4) expand, in apply_tree, to every matching level actually present in the tree, even when those levels are separate sibling nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.PlasmaReaction","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.PlasmaReaction","text":"PlasmaReaction(rate, formula)\n\nA ReactionFormula with a rate attached.\n\nrate is deliberately untyped: a number is a constant rate coefficient, anything callable is evaluated by the consumer (a temperature- or field-dependent fit, an interpolation from a Boltzmann solver). Only scale_rate touches it here.\n\nFor that reason PlasmaReaction has no ==/hash of its own and falls back to identity: a rate is often a closure, and closures compare by identity, so equality of a whole reaction is not well-defined. Compare the formula fields instead – ReactionFormula has proper ==/hash.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.ReactionFormula","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ReactionFormula","text":"ReactionFormula(subs, prods, substoich, prodstoich, reverse)\nReactionFormula(str::AbstractString)\n\nStoichiometry of one reaction: substrate and product species with their coefficients, plus whether it is reversible. No rate — pair it with one using PlasmaReaction.\n\nFields\n\nsubs, prods: Vector{Species}.\nsubstoich, prodstoich: coefficients, Int throughout unless a fractional one appears, which promotes the whole side to Float64.\nreverse::Bool: true for <-->.\n\nThe constructor normalises both sides: repeated species are combined (e + e becomes 2e) and species are sorted, so two formulas written differently but meaning the same thing compare and print identically.\n\n== and hash are field-wise over the normalised form (reverse included), so formulas work as Dict keys and in Set/unique/in. Direction is not canonicalised: a reversible A <--> B is not equal to B <--> A.\n\nFrom a string, see parse_reaction; the @p_str macro is the short form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.apply_tree-Tuple{SpeciesTree, ReactionFormula}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.apply_tree","text":"apply_tree(t::SpeciesTree, r::ReactionFormula)  -> Vector{Tuple{Float64,ReactionFormula}}\napply_tree(t::SpeciesTree, pr::PlasmaReaction)  -> Vector{PlasmaReaction}\n\nExpand a reaction written over lumped species into one reaction per combination of actual states in t.\n\nEvery species in the formula is resolved to the leaves of t it matches, via matching_leaves — an exact state matches itself, a lumped one (N2[X]) matches its whole subtree, and a range (vib=0-4) matches the levels it contains. The result is one reaction per element of the product of those sets.\n\nEach gets a branching factor of 1 / (number of product combinations): the total rate is split equally over the possible outcomes. For a ReactionFormula the factor is returned alongside the reaction; for a PlasmaReaction it is folded into the rate by scale_rate.\n\nReactions whose species are not in the tree at all are dropped with a warning, rather than silently expanding to nothing. Passing several reactions (varargs or a vector) concatenates the results and skips any that throw, again with a warning.\n\njulia> t = SpeciesTree([\"e\", \"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\", \"N2[X,vib=2]\"]);\n\njulia> apply_tree(t, p\"e + N2[X,vib=0-1] --> e + N2[X,vib=2]\")\n2-element Vector{Tuple{Float64, ReactionFormula}}:\n (1.0, e+N2[X,vib=0]-->e+N2[X,vib=2])\n (1.0, e+N2[X,vib=1]-->e+N2[X,vib=2])\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.ischargebalanced-Tuple{ReactionFormula}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ischargebalanced","text":"ischargebalanced(r::ReactionFormula) -> Bool\n\nWhether both sides carry the same total charge. Not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.ismassbalanced-Tuple{ReactionFormula}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ismassbalanced","text":"ismassbalanced(r::ReactionFormula) -> Bool\n\nWhether both sides have the same total mass, to within ≈. Requires every species to have a resolvable mass — errors on a StringGas. Not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.isreactive-Tuple{ReactionFormula}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.isreactive","text":"isreactive(r) -> Bool\n\nWhether a ReactionFormula or PlasmaReaction changes anything: false when both sides hold the same species with the same coefficients. apply_tree can produce such identities when a lumped reaction expands onto a single state, and they contribute nothing to a chemistry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.parse_reaction-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.parse_reaction","text":"parse_reaction(str) -> ReactionFormula\n\nParse a reaction string in LoKI-B notation. Sides are separated by an arrow and terms by +:\n\ne + N2 --> 2e + N2[+]\nN2[X,vib=0] <--> N2[X,vib=1]     # reversible\nN2[X,vib=1] <-- N2[X,vib=0]      # normalised to the forward direction\n\nEach term is an optional stoichiometric coefficient followed by a species string (see Species(::String)). A decimal coefficient (0.5O2[X], common in wall reactions) makes that side Float64. Whitespace is ignored, and a + inside brackets (N2[+]) is not a term separator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.reaction_energy-Tuple{PlasmaReaction}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.reaction_energy","text":"reaction_energy(r::PlasmaReaction) -> Union{Number,Nothing}\n\nEnergy balance Σ E(products) - Σ E(substrates), weighted by stoichiometry. Negative for an exothermic reaction. Returns nothing if any species has no energy set, which is the expected state for a chemistry that has not had apply_energy! applied.\n\nThe unit is whatever energy carries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.scale_rate-Tuple{Number, Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.scale_rate","text":"scale_rate(rate, factor)\n\nScale a reaction rate by a branching factor. A number scales directly; anything else is treated as callable and wrapped, so rate coefficients that depend on temperature or reduced field survive apply_tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.thermal_source_term-Tuple{PlasmaReaction}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.thermal_source_term","text":"thermal_source_term(pr::PlasmaReaction) -> Function\n\nBuild (n₁, n₂, ...) -> rate * ∏ nᵢ^stoichᵢ * (-ΔE), the power released into the gas by pr at the given substrate densities. Arguments are the substrate densities in the order of pr.formula.subs.\n\nSign convention: an exothermic reaction (ΔE < 0, see reaction_energy) gives a positive source. Errors unless every species in the reaction has an energy; set them with apply_energy!.\n\nAssumes a constant rate — the product is formed directly, without evaluating a callable rate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.@p_str-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.@p_str","text":"p\"...\"\n\nParse a species or a reaction, whichever the string looks like: it becomes a ReactionFormula if it contains an arrow, otherwise a Species.\n\np\"N2[X,vib=1]\"            # Species\np\"e + N2 --> 2e + N2[+]\"  # ReactionFormula\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PlasmaSpecies.to_catalyst-Tuple","page":"API","title":"PlasmaSpecies.to_catalyst","text":"to_catalyst(sp::Species)\nto_catalyst(t::SpeciesTree)\nto_catalyst(t::SpeciesTree, reactions...)\n\nExport to Catalyst.jl. Provided by an extension: it only works once Catalyst is loaded in the same session, otherwise every method throws.\n\nA Species becomes a Catalyst species variable named after its string, so anything carrying a charge or a state label is a var\"...\" identifier (var\"N2[+]\", var\"N2[X,vib=1]\").\nA SpeciesTree becomes the sum of its leaf variables.\nreactions are (rate, formula) tuples or PlasmaReactions, given as varargs or a vector. Each is expanded over the tree by apply_tree — branching factors folded into the rate — and returned as a Vector{Reaction} ready for a ReactionSystem. Reactions that throw are skipped with a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"gases/#Gases-and-masses","page":"Gases and masses","title":"Gases and masses","text":"The Gas of a Species is its chemical identity, independent of charge and internal state. A gas label is resolved by Gas(label) in three steps:\n\nREGISTERED_GASES — non-formula labels. Only \"e\", giving Electron.\nparse_formula — a chemical formula, giving a Molecule that knows its composition and therefore its mass.\nStringGas — a label that resolves to neither. It parses, prints and round-trips, but has no composition and no mass.\n\nThere is no registration step: any formula built from the nuclide tables is a usable gas.\n\nusing PlasmaSpecies\ngas(Species(\"CO2\"))\ngas(Species(\"SF6\"))\ngas(Species(\"Xyz\"))","category":"section"},{"location":"gases/#Formula-syntax","page":"Gases and masses","title":"Formula syntax","text":"parse_formula accepts three spellings.\n\nNatural abundance. Bare element symbols, optionally with counts. Each resolves to an Element, i.e. the standard atomic weight — N2 is 2 × 14.007 u, which is what LoKI-B and BOLSIG+ assume for a bulk gas.\n\ncomposition(gas(Species(\"CH4\")))\n\nA single isotopic segment. A mass number in front: \"14N2\", \"16O\".\n\nExoMol iso_slug. Segments joined by -, matching the spelling ExoMol.jl uses, so species strings line up with load_isotopologue slugs.\n\ngas(Species(\"16O-1H\"))\ngas(Species(\"12C-16O2\"))\nstring(gas(Species(\"16O-1H\")))\n\nTwo display forms: print/string is plain ASCII and preserves the written order, so a species round-trips through its string; show is the pretty form with sub- and superscripts.\n\nTwo-letter symbols match greedily, per the usual chemical convention, and case is significant:\n\ngas(Species(\"CO\"))   # carbon monoxide\ngas(Species(\"Co\"))   # cobalt\n\nA formula that does not resolve — unknown symbol, unknown isotope, leftover text — falls back to StringGas rather than throwing. Note that the species parser only accepts letters, digits and - in the gas token, so a label like \"my_gas\" is a parse error, while \"mygas\" is a StringGas.","category":"section"},{"location":"gases/#Composition-and-equality","page":"Gases and masses","title":"Composition and equality","text":"A Molecule stores the composition twice: as written, and canonical (repeated nuclides merged, sorted by atomic and mass number). == and hash use the canonical form, so OH and HO are the same gas and the same Dict key, while display keeps what was written.\n\ngas(Species(\"OH\")) == gas(Species(\"HO\"))\ncomposition(gas(Species(\"H2O\")))\ncanonical_composition(gas(Species(\"H2O\")))","category":"section"},{"location":"gases/#Masses","page":"Gases and masses","title":"Masses","text":"mass is in kilograms throughout. For a species, the electrons implied by the charge are added or removed.\n\nmass(Species(\"CO2\")) / PlasmaSpecies.U_KG   # in daltons\nmass(Species(\"28Si-16O\")) / PlasmaSpecies.U_KG\nmass(Species(\"N2[+]\")) == mass(Species(\"N2\")) - mass(Species(\"e\"))","category":"section"},{"location":"gases/#Nuclide-data","page":"Gases and masses","title":"Nuclide data","text":"Two tables, both in src/nuclide_data.jl:\n\nELEMENTS — all 118 elements, standard atomic weights (IUPAC/CIAAW 2021 conventional values). Elements with no stable nuclide carry the mass number of their longest-lived isotope.\nISOTOPES — exact nuclide masses (AME2020), keyed by (symbol, mass number). Not the full nuclide chart: it covers the elements that come up in plasma chemistry and molecular spectroscopy. Adding one is a single line.\n\nELEMENT_ALIASES maps symbols that stand for a specific nuclide — D and T — so \"D2\" and \"HT\" resolve without a mass number.\n\nPlasmaSpecies.element(:D)\nmass(Species(\"D2\")) / PlasmaSpecies.U_KG\n\nLook-ups are element and isotope, both returning nothing for anything unknown.","category":"section"},{"location":"gases/#Gases-without-a-formula","page":"Gases and masses","title":"Gases without a formula","text":"A StringGas behaves like any other gas except that it cannot be weighed:\n\nmass(Species(\"Xyz\"))\n\nIf a label needs a mass anyway, define a gas type and a mass method for it:\n\nstruct UnknownGas <: PlasmaSpecies.Gas end\nPlasmaSpecies.mass(::UnknownGas) = π * 1e-26\nmass(Species(UnknownGas()))","category":"section"},{"location":"#PlasmaSpecies.jl","page":"Home","title":"PlasmaSpecies.jl","text":"Bookkeeping for the species and reactions of a plasma-chemistry model.\n\nPlasmaSpecies.jl gives one concrete answer to \"what is a species, and when are two of them the same\" — then builds the hierarchy, reaction parsing and export on top of it. It does no physics: no rate coefficients, no transport, no solver.","category":"section"},{"location":"#What-it-provides","page":"Home","title":"What it provides","text":"Species — a gas, a charge, and optionally an electronic, vibrational and rotational state. Written in the string notation of the LoKI-B Boltzmann solver (\"N2[+,B,vib=3]\"), with a p\"...\" macro for both species and reactions.\nGases resolved from chemical formulas — anything built from known nuclides works without registration and knows its own mass, natural-abundance (\"CO2\") or isotopically specific (\"12C-16O2\").\nSpeciesTree — the parent/child hierarchy relating bulk N2 to N2[X] to N2[X,vib=1], with the leaves as the states a model integrates.\nReactionFormula / PlasmaReaction — parsed reactions, reversible and fractional coefficients included, and apply_tree to expand one written over lumped species into one per resolved state.\nto_catalyst — export to Catalyst.jl reaction networks, via a package extension.","category":"section"},{"location":"#Installation","page":"Home","title":"Installation","text":"julia> import Pkg; Pkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/jqfeld/PlasmaSpecies.jl.git\")","category":"section"},{"location":"#A-first-look","page":"Home","title":"A first look","text":"using PlasmaSpecies\nsp = p\"N2[+,B]\"\ngas(sp), charge(sp), electronic_state(sp)\nmass(sp)\nr = p\"e + N2 --> 2e + N2[+]\"\nt = SpeciesTree([\"e\", \"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\", \"N2[+]\"])\nleaves(t)\napply_tree(t, p\"e + N2[X] --> 2e + N2[+]\")","category":"section"},{"location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","title":"Where to go next","text":"Page Covers\nSpecies the string notation, fields, identity, state matching\nGases and masses formula parsing, isotopes, nuclide data\nSpecies trees building and querying the hierarchy\nReactions parsing, expansion over a tree, energetics, Catalyst\nAPI every exported name","category":"section"},{"location":"reactions/#Reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"Reactions","text":"Two types. A ReactionFormula is stoichiometry only — which species, which coefficients, reversible or not. A PlasmaReaction is a formula with a rate attached.","category":"section"},{"location":"reactions/#Parsing","page":"Reactions","title":"Parsing","text":"using PlasmaSpecies\nr = p\"e + N2 --> 2e + N2[+]\"\nr.subs, r.substoich\nr.prods, r.prodstoich\nr.reverse\n\nThe p\"...\" macro returns a ReactionFormula when the string contains an arrow and a Species otherwise; parse_reaction is the function form. Terms are separated by +, and a + inside brackets (N2[+]) is not a separator.\n\nThree arrows:\n\np\"N2[X,vib=0] <--> N2[X,vib=1]\"     # reversible, sets r.reverse\nparse_reaction(\"N2[X,vib=1] <-- N2[X,vib=0]\")   # normalised to forward\n\nCoefficients may be fractional, which is common for wall and surface reactions. A decimal anywhere on a side promotes that side's coefficients to Float64; otherwise they stay Int:\n\nf = parse_reaction(\"O[X] + 0.5O2[X] --> O3\")\nf.substoich\n\nBoth sides are normalised on construction: repeated species are combined and species are sorted, so formulas that mean the same thing print and compare the same.\n\nparse_reaction(\"e + e + N2 --> N2\")","category":"section"},{"location":"reactions/#Expanding-over-a-tree","page":"Reactions","title":"Expanding over a tree","text":"A chemistry is usually written over lumped species and solved over resolved ones. apply_tree does the expansion: every species in the formula is resolved to the leaves it matches, and one reaction is emitted per combination.\n\nt = SpeciesTree([\"e\", \"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\", \"N2[X,vib=2]\", \"N2[+]\"])\napply_tree(t, p\"e + N2[X,vib=0-1] --> e + N2[X,vib=2]\")\n\nEach result carries a branching factor of 1 / (number of product combinations): the rate is split equally over the possible outcomes. Writing a product as a lumped species therefore distributes it:\n\napply_tree(t, p\"e + N2[X,vib=0] --> e + N2[X]\")\n\nFor a PlasmaReaction the factor is folded into the rate by scale_rate instead, and the result is a vector of PlasmaReaction:\n\npr = PlasmaReaction(1.5e-16, p\"e + N2[X,vib=0-1] --> e + N2[X,vib=2]\")\n[(x.rate, x.formula) for x in apply_tree(t, pr)]\n\nA species that matches nothing in the tree would silently expand the reaction away, so instead the reaction is dropped with a warning. Passing several reactions concatenates the results and skips any that throw.\n\nExpansion can produce identities (A --> A) when a lumped reaction collapses onto one state. isreactive filters them out.","category":"section"},{"location":"reactions/#Rates","page":"Reactions","title":"Rates","text":"PlasmaReaction.rate is deliberately untyped. A number is a constant rate coefficient; anything callable is left to the consumer to evaluate, so a fit in gas temperature or reduced field, or an interpolation from a Boltzmann solver, works unchanged. scale_rate is the only place this package touches a rate, and it handles both:\n\nscale_rate(2.0, 0.5)\nscale_rate(Te -> 1e-15 * Te^0.5, 0.5)(4.0)","category":"section"},{"location":"reactions/#Energetics","page":"Reactions","title":"Energetics","text":"Give the species energies — usually with apply_energy! over a tree — and a reaction's energy balance follows:\n\ntv = SpeciesTree([\"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\"]);\napply_energy!(tv, q -> 0.29 * q.vib);\nlv = leaves(tv)\npr = PlasmaReaction(2.0, ReactionFormula([lv[1]], [lv[2]], [1], [1], false))\nreaction_energy(pr)\nthermal_source_term(pr)(1e20)\n\nreaction_energy is Σ E(products) - Σ E(substrates), negative for an exothermic reaction, and nothing if any energy is unset. thermal_source_term builds the gas-heating term rate * ∏ nᵢ^stoichᵢ * (-ΔE) as a function of the substrate densities, in the order of pr.formula.subs.\n\nTwo consistency checks are available, though not exported: PlasmaSpecies.ismassbalanced and PlasmaSpecies.ischargebalanced.\n\nPlasmaSpecies.ischargebalanced(p\"e + N2 --> 2e + N2[+]\")","category":"section"},{"location":"reactions/#Export-to-Catalyst","page":"Reactions","title":"Export to Catalyst","text":"Loading Catalyst.jl activates an extension providing to_catalyst. Species variables are named after the species string, so anything with a charge or state label becomes a var\"...\" identifier.\n\njulia> using PlasmaSpecies, Catalyst\n\njulia> t = SpeciesTree([\"e\", \"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\", \"N2[+]\"]);\n\njulia> rs = to_catalyst(t, (1.0, p\"e + N2[X] --> 2e + N2[+]\"))\n2-element Vector{Reaction{...}}:\n 1.0, e + var\"N2[X,vib=0]\" --> 2*e + var\"N2[+]\"\n 1.0, e + var\"N2[X,vib=1]\" --> 2*e + var\"N2[+]\"\n\njulia> ReactionSystem(rs, default_t(); name = :plasma)\nModel plasma:\nUnknowns (4): see unknowns(plasma)\n  e(t)\n  var\"N2[X,vib=0]\"(t)\n  var\"N2[+]\"(t)\n  var\"N2[X,vib=1]\"(t)\n\nReactions are given as (rate, formula) tuples or PlasmaReactions, singly, as varargs or as a vector, and are expanded over the tree on the way out. A SpeciesTree on its own converts to the sum of its leaf variables.","category":"section"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.ReactionFormula-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.ReactionFormula","text":"ReactionFormula(subs, prods, substoich, prodstoich, reverse)\nReactionFormula(str::AbstractString)\n\nStoichiometry of one reaction: substrate and product species with their coefficients, plus whether it is reversible. No rate — pair it with one using PlasmaReaction.\n\nFields\n\nsubs, prods: Vector{Species}.\nsubstoich, prodstoich: coefficients, Int throughout unless a fractional one appears, which promotes the whole side to Float64.\nreverse::Bool: true for <-->.\n\nThe constructor normalises both sides: repeated species are combined (e + e becomes 2e) and species are sorted, so two formulas written differently but meaning the same thing compare and print identically.\n\n== and hash are field-wise over the normalised form (reverse included), so formulas work as Dict keys and in Set/unique/in. Direction is not canonicalised: a reversible A <--> B is not equal to B <--> A.\n\nFrom a string, see parse_reaction; the @p_str macro is the short form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.PlasmaReaction-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.PlasmaReaction","text":"PlasmaReaction(rate, formula)\n\nA ReactionFormula with a rate attached.\n\nrate is deliberately untyped: a number is a constant rate coefficient, anything callable is evaluated by the consumer (a temperature- or field-dependent fit, an interpolation from a Boltzmann solver). Only scale_rate touches it here.\n\nFor that reason PlasmaReaction has no ==/hash of its own and falls back to identity: a rate is often a closure, and closures compare by identity, so equality of a whole reaction is not well-defined. Compare the formula fields instead – ReactionFormula has proper ==/hash.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.parse_reaction-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.parse_reaction","text":"parse_reaction(str) -> ReactionFormula\n\nParse a reaction string in LoKI-B notation. Sides are separated by an arrow and terms by +:\n\ne + N2 --> 2e + N2[+]\nN2[X,vib=0] <--> N2[X,vib=1]     # reversible\nN2[X,vib=1] <-- N2[X,vib=0]      # normalised to the forward direction\n\nEach term is an optional stoichiometric coefficient followed by a species string (see Species(::String)). A decimal coefficient (0.5O2[X], common in wall reactions) makes that side Float64. Whitespace is ignored, and a + inside brackets (N2[+]) is not a term separator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.@p_str-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.@p_str","text":"p\"...\"\n\nParse a species or a reaction, whichever the string looks like: it becomes a ReactionFormula if it contains an arrow, otherwise a Species.\n\np\"N2[X,vib=1]\"            # Species\np\"e + N2 --> 2e + N2[+]\"  # ReactionFormula\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.apply_tree-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.apply_tree","text":"apply_tree(t::SpeciesTree, r::ReactionFormula)  -> Vector{Tuple{Float64,ReactionFormula}}\napply_tree(t::SpeciesTree, pr::PlasmaReaction)  -> Vector{PlasmaReaction}\n\nExpand a reaction written over lumped species into one reaction per combination of actual states in t.\n\nEvery species in the formula is resolved to the leaves of t it matches, via matching_leaves — an exact state matches itself, a lumped one (N2[X]) matches its whole subtree, and a range (vib=0-4) matches the levels it contains. The result is one reaction per element of the product of those sets.\n\nEach gets a branching factor of 1 / (number of product combinations): the total rate is split equally over the possible outcomes. For a ReactionFormula the factor is returned alongside the reaction; for a PlasmaReaction it is folded into the rate by scale_rate.\n\nReactions whose species are not in the tree at all are dropped with a warning, rather than silently expanding to nothing. Passing several reactions (varargs or a vector) concatenates the results and skips any that throw, again with a warning.\n\njulia> t = SpeciesTree([\"e\", \"N2[X,vib=0]\", \"N2[X,vib=1]\", \"N2[X,vib=2]\"]);\n\njulia> apply_tree(t, p\"e + N2[X,vib=0-1] --> e + N2[X,vib=2]\")\n2-element Vector{Tuple{Float64, ReactionFormula}}:\n (1.0, e+N2[X,vib=0]-->e+N2[X,vib=2])\n (1.0, e+N2[X,vib=1]-->e+N2[X,vib=2])\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.scale_rate-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.scale_rate","text":"scale_rate(rate, factor)\n\nScale a reaction rate by a branching factor. A number scales directly; anything else is treated as callable and wrapped, so rate coefficients that depend on temperature or reduced field survive apply_tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.isreactive-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.isreactive","text":"isreactive(r) -> Bool\n\nWhether a ReactionFormula or PlasmaReaction changes anything: false when both sides hold the same species with the same coefficients. apply_tree can produce such identities when a lumped reaction expands onto a single state, and they contribute nothing to a chemistry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.reaction_energy-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.reaction_energy","text":"reaction_energy(r::PlasmaReaction) -> Union{Number,Nothing}\n\nEnergy balance Σ E(products) - Σ E(substrates), weighted by stoichiometry. Negative for an exothermic reaction. Returns nothing if any species has no energy set, which is the expected state for a chemistry that has not had apply_energy! applied.\n\nThe unit is whatever energy carries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.thermal_source_term-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.thermal_source_term","text":"thermal_source_term(pr::PlasmaReaction) -> Function\n\nBuild (n₁, n₂, ...) -> rate * ∏ nᵢ^stoichᵢ * (-ΔE), the power released into the gas by pr at the given substrate densities. Arguments are the substrate densities in the order of pr.formula.subs.\n\nSign convention: an exothermic reaction (ΔE < 0, see reaction_energy) gives a positive source. Errors unless every species in the reaction has an energy; set them with apply_energy!.\n\nAssumes a constant rate — the product is formed directly, without evaluating a callable rate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reactions/#PlasmaSpecies.to_catalyst-reactions","page":"Reactions","title":"PlasmaSpecies.to_catalyst","text":"to_catalyst(sp::Species)\nto_catalyst(t::SpeciesTree)\nto_catalyst(t::SpeciesTree, reactions...)\n\nExport to Catalyst.jl. Provided by an extension: it only works once Catalyst is loaded in the same session, otherwise every method throws.\n\nA Species becomes a Catalyst species variable named after its string, so anything carrying a charge or a state label is a var\"...\" identifier (var\"N2[+]\", var\"N2[X,vib=1]\").\nA SpeciesTree becomes the sum of its leaf variables.\nreactions are (rate, formula) tuples or PlasmaReactions, given as varargs or a vector. Each is expanded over the tree by apply_tree — branching factors folded into the rate — and returned as a Vector{Reaction} ready for a ReactionSystem. Reactions that throw are skipped with a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"species/#Species-and-states","page":"Species","title":"Species and states","text":"A Species is one state of one gas: a Gas, a Charge, and optionally an electronic, vibrational and rotational state.","category":"section"},{"location":"species/#The-string-notation","page":"Species","title":"The string notation","text":"Species are normally written as strings, in the notation of the LoKI-B Boltzmann solver:\n\nGAS[CHARGE,ELECTRONIC,vib=VIB,rot=ROT]\n\nEvery bracketed field is optional, but the order is fixed.\n\nField Syntax Becomes\nGAS N2, CO2, 16O-1H, e a Gas — see Gases and masses\nCHARGE +, ++, -, 0, omitted Positive, Negative, Neutral\nELECTRONIC X, a'', B3Pig an ElectronicState\nvib=, rot= 0, 0-4, (0,1,0), (v1=1,v2=0), asym a QuantumLabel\n\nusing PlasmaSpecies\nSpecies(\"e\")\nSpecies(\"N2[+]\")\nSpecies(\"N2[+,B]\")\nSpecies(\"N2[X,vib=0,rot=10]\")\np\"N4[+]\"\n\nThe p\"...\" macro parses a species or a reaction, whichever the string looks like. Whitespace is stripped, and a + inside brackets is never mistaken for a charge separator.\n\nA malformed string throws rather than parsing the part it recognises — \"N2[+\" is a typo, not a neutral N₂.\n\nSpecies(\"N2[+\")","category":"section"},{"location":"species/#Quantum-labels","page":"Species","title":"Quantum labels","text":"vib=/rot= accept more than a single quantum number. The forms are collected in QuantumLabel and parsed by parse_quantum_label:\n\nvibrational_state(Species(\"N2[X,vib=3]\"))          # one level\nvibrational_state(Species(\"N2[X,vib=0-4]\"))        # a lumped range\nvibrational_state(Species(\"CO2[X,vib=(0,1,0)]\"))   # normal modes\nvibrational_state(Species(\"CO2[X,vib=(v1=1,v2=0)]\"))\nvibrational_state(Species(\"N2[X,vib=asym]\"))       # opaque label\n\nA range is the one form with containment semantics: vib=0-4 matches the levels inside it when a reaction is expanded over a tree. A Symbol label is deliberately opaque and only ever matches itself.\n\nDisplay round-trips: Species(string(sp)) == sp for every form above.","category":"section"},{"location":"species/#Electronic-states","page":"Species","title":"Electronic states","text":"A plain letter label becomes a StringElectronicState. A fused term-symbol string, in the spelling ExoMol's ElecState column uses, decomposes into a TermSymbol:\n\nelectronic_state(Species(\"N2[+,B]\"))\nts = ElectronicState(\"A3Sigmau+\")\nts.multiplicity, ts.Λ, ts.parity, ts.reflection\n\nBoth compare by label alone, so a TermSymbol and a bare label for the same state are equal and interchangeable when matching. The extra physics is annotation.","category":"section"},{"location":"species/#Fields-and-accessors","page":"Species","title":"Fields and accessors","text":"sp = Species(\"N2[+,B]\")\ngas(sp)\ncharge(sp)\nelectronic_state(sp)\nvibrational_state(sp) === nothing\n\nnothing means unresolved, not zero. N2 is bulk N₂ and N2[X,vib=0] is its ground vibrational level; they are different species, related by a SpeciesTree.\n\nThree further fields carry data about a state rather than naming it: energy, degeneracy and metadata. Set them with with_fields, which copies a species and replaces only the named fields:\n\nexcited = with_fields(sp, energy = 11.03, degeneracy = 3)\nenergy(excited), degeneracy(excited)\n\nSpecies are immutable, so this is the only way to change one.","category":"section"},{"location":"species/#Identity","page":"Species","title":"Identity","text":"== and hash use the identity fields only — gas, charge, and the three state fields. energy, degeneracy and metadata are ignored:\n\nsp == excited\nhash(sp) == hash(excited)\nd = Dict(sp => \"N2+ in B\"); d[excited]\n\nThat is deliberate. Attaching an energy or a downstream payload must not break a Dict{Species,V} lookup, which is how consumers index their state vectors.\n\nSpecies also sort (isless is defined for them): electrons last, then by charge, gas, electronic state (with X first), and level. Sorting is total and never throws, even for mixed label types.","category":"section"},{"location":"species/#Matching-states","page":"Species","title":"Matching states","text":"Matching is separate from equality: a species with nothing or a range in a field acts as a pattern for the more resolved states under it.\n\nSpecies(\"N2[X,vib=1]\") in Species(\"N2[X]\")        # wildcard field\nSpecies(\"N2[X,vib=2]\") in Species(\"N2[X,vib=0-4]\") # range contains level\nSpecies(\"N2[X]\") in Species(\"N2[X]\")               # reflexive\nis_parent_species(Species(\"N2[X]\"), Species(\"N2[X]\"))  # strict: not its own parent\n\nUse in/species_matches to ask \"does this pattern cover that state\", and is_parent_species for hierarchy construction, where a species must not be its own ancestor. get_parent_species walks one step up:\n\nget_parent_species(Species(\"N2[X,vib=1]\"))\nget_parent_species(Species(\"N2\"))","category":"section"},{"location":"species/#Mass","page":"Species","title":"Mass","text":"mass returns kg, derived from the gas composition, with the electrons implied by the charge added or removed:\n\nmass(p\"N2\")\nmass(p\"N2[+]\") == mass(p\"N2\") - mass(p\"e\")\nmass(p\"e\")\n\nAny formula built from the nuclide tables works — no registration step. A label that is not a resolvable formula still parses and round-trips, but has no mass:\n\ngas(Species(\"Xyz\"))\nmass(Species(\"Xyz\"))\n\nSee Gases and masses for formula syntax, isotopes and the data tables.","category":"section"},{"location":"species/#PlasmaSpecies.Species-species","page":"Species","title":"PlasmaSpecies.Species","text":"Species(; gas, charge=Neutral(), electronic_state=nothing,\n          vibrational_state=nothing, rotational_state=nothing,\n          energy=nothing, degeneracy=nothing, metadata=nothing)\n\nOne state of one gas. All fields are type parameters, so a Species is concretely typed and cheap to store in arrays and Dict keys.\n\nUsually built from a LoKI-B string instead — see Species(::String).\n\nIdentity fields\n\nCompared by == and hashed. Together they name the state:\n\ngas: the parent gas. Normally a Molecule resolved from a chemical formula (see parse_formula), otherwise Electron or the StringGas fallback.\ncharge: Neutral (default), Positive(n) or Negative(n).\nelectronic_state: an ElectronicState, or nothing for unresolved.\nvibrational_state, rotational_state: a QuantumLabel, or nothing. By convention a resolved level implies its parent is resolved too (rot without vib is meaningless), but this is not enforced.\n\nnothing means unresolved, not zero: N2 is bulk N₂, N2[X,vib=0] is its ground vibrational level, and the two are different species that a SpeciesTree relates as parent and child.\n\nPayload fields\n\nIgnored by == and hash, carried through with_fields:\n\nenergy: level energy, used by reaction_energy. Set in bulk with apply_energy!.\ndegeneracy: statistical weight of the level.\nmetadata: opaque payload for downstream packages.\n\nThe split is deliberate: energy/degeneracy/metadata are annotations on a state, not part of its identity, so attaching them never breaks a Dict{Species,V} lookup.\n\nAn Electron gas always carries Negative(1); any charge passed for one is normalised to that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"species/#PlasmaSpecies.level_matches-species","page":"Species","title":"PlasmaSpecies.level_matches","text":"level_matches(formula_value, candidate_value) -> Bool\n\nWhether a single field (electronic/vibrational/rotational state) of a formula Species matches a concrete candidate value. nothing on the formula side is a wildcard (matches anything, as used by is_parent_species for hierarchy matching); a UnitRange{Int} on the formula side matches any Int it contains (used by matching_leaves to expand reactions written over a range of vibrational levels); everything else falls back to exact equality — including Symbol labels, which are deliberately opaque (no containment).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"species/#PlasmaSpecies.species_matches-species","page":"Species","title":"PlasmaSpecies.species_matches","text":"species_matches(formula::Species, candidate::Species) -> Bool\n\nWhether candidate is matched by formula: gas/charge compared exactly, electronic/vibrational/rotational state compared via level_matches (so nothing/range fields on formula act as wildcards/containment checks). Used by matching_leaves to expand a reaction formula species — which may specify a range of vibrational levels — into every matching leaf of a SpeciesTree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"species/#Base.in-species","page":"Species","title":"Base.in","text":"candidate::Species in formula::Species -> Bool\n\nWhether candidate is matched by formula — an alias for species_matches(formula, candidate), exposed via ∈/in to mirror SpectraUtils's Base.in(child::State, parent::State) (e.g. s in boltzmann.parent). nothing/range fields on formula act as wildcards/ containment checks, same as species_matches; reflexive (sp in sp is true), unlike is_parent_species which is a strict-ancestor check and doesn't compare rotational_state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"species/#PlasmaSpecies.is_parent_species-species","page":"Species","title":"PlasmaSpecies.is_parent_species","text":"is_parent_species(sp::Species, parent::Species) -> Bool\n\nWhether parent is a strict ancestor of sp in the species hierarchy: same gas and charge, electronic and vibrational state matched via level_matches (so nothing on parent acts as a wildcard), and parent != sp. rotational_state is deliberately not compared. Unlike species_matches, this is irreflexive — a species is not its own parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"}]
}
